From a464dd59f8d70b2684b07c286fe4dca0c208c106 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 09:43:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] reaper v2.4.2: Warden outbound + self-filter, FQDN via
 dnsmasq, firewall help, two field fixes

Warden
  - direction out/both blocked nothing: `rw_allow src -> RETURN` preceded the
    destination rules, and every outbound packet has a LAN host as its source,
    so any whitelist entry covering that host (including the <lan>/24 entry the
    lockout guidance tells people to keep) returned out of the chain before a
    single dst rule ran. Inbound was unaffected, which is the reported asymmetry.
    The dst group moves into its own RW_OUT chain, reached before any src RETURN,
    and gains the loopback/LAN/own-WAN exemptions the dst side never had.
  - new opt-in RW_SELF chain on OUTPUT (nvram rwarden_self, default 0) so a
    compromised router cannot beacon to a flagged address. Drops go to RW_SDROP,
    which always logs. Upstream resolvers (/tmp/resolv.dnsmasq) and NTP are
    exempt; cannot lock the operator out, since management is INPUT and replies
    carry a LAN dst. rwatch reports self-drops.
  - the block total and the country table could never reconcile: the total is
    every drop, the table only the geo rules, and the feed rule is evaluated
    first (same drop target - an attribution order, not an enforcement one).
    THREAT/BAN are now banked alongside TOTAL/CC and the page shows the split.

Firewall
  - FQDN objects populate from dnsmasq `ipset=` (rfw_gen_dnsmasq/rfw_sync_dnsmasq,
    spliced in services.c before append_custom_config). The recorded blocker -
    "this dnsmasq is compiled without ipset" - was a misreading of a stock MIPS
    binary from another model; no rebuild was needed. FQDN sets gain timeout 3600
    and are no longer flushed on apply. rfw_gen_resolve stays as the floor.
  - every tab gains an explainer and a help button into docs/FIREWALL-GUIDE.md.

Field reports
  - Traffic per-network view labelled raw bridges; now name + VLAN alongside the
    bridge, from get_mtlan(), published as a side table so the persisted history
    key is unchanged.
  - Wi-Fi clients on AiMesh nodes read as Wired: neither the local assoclists nor
    the bridge FDB can see them, so the backhaul port decided it. rdev_scan_amesh
    consults /tmp/allwclientlist.json, narrowed so first-hand radio data always
    wins and a stale wireless entry loses to the wired list.

i18n: RWDN_69-76 and RFW_229-251 English-seeded, 25 packs lockstep.
Not yet validated on hardware.
