@@ -6547,6 +6555,29 @@ RFW_225=Firewall engine enabled
 RFW_226=Firewall engine disabled
 RFW_227=Still working. Check the System Log if this persists.
 RFW_228=Empty until you click Export, or paste a saved configuration here to restore it.
+RFW_229=Open the guide for this tab. Opens in a new browser tab.
+RFW_230=A read-only picture of what is filtering traffic right now: whether the base firewall and the Reaper engine are actually up, how much of your configuration is loaded, and what of this router can be reached from the internet. Check it after every apply and after a reboot - what you configured and what is live can differ while a restart is in flight.
+RFW_231=Example: you add a port forward and the table shows it, but this tab still reports the engine inactive. The rule exists in your configuration and is doing nothing until that changes.
+RFW_232=The base firewall: the master switch, DoS protection, whether the router answers pings from the internet, what gets logged, and an allow-list of outside addresses permitted to reach the router itself. The IPv6 firewall here is genuinely separate - IPv6 is not translated, so every device has a globally routable address and only that switch stands in front of it.
+RFW_233=Example: to let only your office reach the web interface from outside, enable the WAN access allow-list and add one entry - source 203.0.113.10, port 8443, TCP. Every other source is refused whatever the port forwards say.
+RFW_234=The rules engine. Each rule names a direction, an action, an optional source and destination as zones or objects, an optional service, schedule and rate limit. Define your objects and zones first, then write rules that refer to them by name. First match wins, so put specific exceptions above broad blocks. Use Preview to see the exact commands before applying, and press Keep only once you have confirmed you can still reach the router.
+RFW_235=Example: to keep a TV off the internet during the working day - direction Forward, action Drop, source object tv, destination zone wan, schedule Mon,Tue,Wed,Thu,Fri|09:00|17:00. Leave the service empty so it covers every protocol.
+RFW_236=Named things to point rules at, so a rule reads printer instead of an address you have to remember. Address objects cover a host or subnet, a range, a MAC address, a domain name or a country; groups bundle several under one name; service objects name a protocol and its ports. Name them after what they are, not where they are - then moving a device means editing the object once instead of hunting through rules.
+RFW_237=Example: an address object nas of type host with value 192.168.50.20, plus a service object smb with protocol TCP and ports 139,445. A rule blocking smb to nas from your guest zone then needs no comment to explain it.
+RFW_238=A zone is a name for a set of interfaces, and a zone policy says what happens by default when traffic moves from one zone to another. Zones let you write intent once instead of repeating interface names in every rule. Set the broad policy here, then write specific rules for the exceptions. Interface names accept a trailing + as a wildcard.
+RFW_239=Example: zone guest on interface br1, zone lan on br0, then a policy of source guest, destination lan, action Drop. Guests reach the internet but nothing on your home network, and you have not written a single address.
+RFW_240=A per-device default for traffic leaving your network toward the internet - the short path for this device should not be online, without writing a full rule. It overrides the zone policy but loses to an explicit rule, so you can block a device here and still allow one thing through above it. Prefer Reject for devices someone is sitting at, so the application fails immediately instead of hanging; Drop reveals less and suits devices nobody is watching.
+RFW_241=Example: object kids-tablet, action Reject, schedule Sun,Mon,Tue,Wed,Thu 21:00-07:00. The tablet still reaches the printer and a local media server overnight, but not the internet.
+RFW_242=Port forwarding: traffic arriving from the internet on a chosen port is sent to a device inside your network. A forward is a hole in the firewall by definition, so make it as small as it can be - restrict the source whenever you know who will connect, and give the target device a fixed address first or the forward will eventually point somewhere else. Export writes the whole firewall configuration out as text you can paste back to restore.
+RFW_243=Example: protocol TCP, external port 8443, internal address 192.168.50.80, internal port 443, source restricted to an object holding your office address. Everyone else scanning that port finds nothing.
+RFW_244=The long-standing service filter: it matches traffic leaving your LAN by address, port and protocol within a daily time window. Choose the mode deliberately - a block-list blocks what you list and allows the rest, while an allow-list permits only what you list and will cut off anything you forgot. The window applies to the whole list, not per entry; for per-device scheduling use the Rules or Egress tabs instead.
+RFW_245=Example: to stop devices using outside DNS servers - mode Block-list, source and destination blank, destination port 53, protocol UDP, then a second entry for TCP. The router own resolver still works because that traffic never leaves the LAN.
+RFW_246=Blocks web requests whose address contains one of the keywords you list. Keep entries short and distinctive, and test one before adding a long list. Be realistic about the limit: this reads the request as it passes, so it works on unencrypted requests and misses almost everything on the modern web. Treat it as a nudge for casual use - for blocking that holds, use a domain-name object in a rule, or block at the DNS level.
+RFW_247=Example: the entry example-game.com refuses plain requests to that domain, and does nothing to the same site over HTTPS.
+RFW_248=Blocks web pages whose content contains one of the words you list. The same limit applies and more so - the filter has to be able to read the page to match a word in it, so encrypted sites pass untouched. It also matches inside ordinary text, so a short or common word will block pages you did not intend. Prefer distinctive phrases and add them one at a time.
+RFW_249=Example: the entry freegamedownload blocks a plain page containing that string, and misses one that is encrypted or spells it differently.
+RFW_250=Chooses which firewall decisions are written to the system log and shows recent entries. Leave it off for normal running - a busy connection can log thousands of lines a minute and push out everything else worth reading. Turn on Drop while diagnosing a rule that is not behaving, read the entries, then turn it back off.
+RFW_251=Example: a device cannot reach a service and you do not know which rule stops it. Set logging to Drop, reproduce the failure, and read the entries - each line names the addresses and ports, which identifies the rule that matched.
 RDEV_88=Export list
 RDEV_89=Export
 RDEV_90=The file contains real device names, MAC and IP addresses. Keep it somewhere private - it is not safe to post in a forum or attach to a bug report.
